<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-expanding" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Expanding Brackets</title>
<introduction>
  <p>Start with the mathematics: the <xref ref="glossary-distributive-law" text="title"/><idx>distributive law</idx>. It explains why expansion works. Splitting brackets, FOIL, diagrams and column multiplication are ways of using the same law, not different laws.</p>
  <p>We begin with one term multiplying a bracket, then move to two brackets. Understand the principle first; choose an accurate and efficient method as you become confident.</p>
  <p>Before you start, revise signed numbers, coefficients, powers and like terms in <xref ref="algebra-vocabulary"/> if needed.</p>
</introduction>
<section xml:id="expanding-principle">
<title>The mathematics: the distributive law</title>
<p>Brackets indicate <term>grouping</term>. Grouping occurs elsewhere in mathematics too: the numerator and denominator of a fraction are each groups, and the expression beneath a square-root sign is a group.</p>
<p>Multiplication <term>distributes</term> over addition and subtraction:</p>
<me>a(b+c)=ab+ac.</me>
<me>a(b-c)=ab-ac.</me>
<p>Instead of adding or subtracting within the brackets first, we may multiply each term by the factor outside. Sometimes the terms inside cannot be combined because they are unlike terms. The distributive law still applies.</p>
<figure xml:id="figure-expanding-distributive-law">
<caption>One factor multiplies every term in a group.</caption>
<image source="diagrams/distributive-law.svg" width="78%">
<shortdescription>Two arrows show that 3 multiplies both x and 4 in 3(x+4), giving 3x+12.</shortdescription>
<description><p>The expression <m>3(x+4)</m> is above two arrows originating from the factor <m>3</m>. The first arrow leads to <m>3\cdot x</m> and the second to <m>3\cdot 4</m>. These partial products combine to give <m>3x+12</m>.</p></description>
</image>
</figure>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-principle-versus-method">
<title>Principles and methods</title>
<p>The distributive law is a <term>mathematical principle</term>. It explains why the expressions are equal, regardless of how we arrange our calculations.</p>
<p>A <term>method</term> is a way of applying that principle. Several methods may perform the same calculation, each with its own advantages.</p>
<p>One aim of learning mathematics is to develop <xref ref="glossary-strategic-competence" text="title"/><idx>strategic competence</idx>: knowing different approaches, understanding how they work, and choosing an appropriate method for the problem.</p>
<p>Visualisation matters. Diagrams and carefully arranged calculations can reveal mathematical structure that is less obvious when everything is written on one line.</p>
<p>In the following sections, we will explore several methods. <em>The mathematics remains the same; what changes is how we organise and see it.</em></p>
</paragraphs>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-monomial-bracket">
<title>One term multiplying a bracket</title>
<p>A <term>monomial</term> is an expression containing one term, such as <m>3</m>, <m>-2x</m> or <m>4x^2</m>. Begin by multiplying a monomial by every term in a bracket.</p>
<example xml:id="expanding-monomial-first">
<title>Multiply both terms</title>
<statement><p>Expand <m>3(x+4)</m>.</p></statement>
<solution>
<me>3(x+4)=3\cdot x+3\cdot4=3x+12.</me>
<p>Both <m>x</m> and <m>4</m> are multiplied by <m>3</m>.</p>
</solution>
</example>
<example xml:id="expanding-monomial-variable">
<title>A variable outside the bracket</title>
<statement><p>Expand <m>2x(3x+5)</m>.</p></statement>
<solution><me>2x(3x+5)=6x^2+10x.</me>
<p>Since <m>x\cdot x=x^2</m>, the first product is <m>6x^2</m>.</p></solution>
</example>
<example xml:id="expanding-monomial-negative">
<title>Keep the negative sign</title>
<statement><p>Expand <m>-3x(2x-4)</m>.</p></statement>
<solution><me>-3x(2x-4)=-6x^2+12x.</me>
<p>The second product is positive because the product of two negative numbers is positive.</p></solution>
</example>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-monomial-predict">
<statement><p>Before calculating <m>5(x+7)</m>, predict the coefficient of <m>x</m> and the constant term. Then expand.</p></statement>
<answer><p>The coefficient is <m>5</m>, and the constant term is <m>35</m>. So <m>5(x+7)=5x+35</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-monomial-signs">
<statement><p>Expand <m>4(2x-3)</m>, <m>-2(x+5)</m>, and <m>-3(2x-4)</m>. Check each sign.</p></statement>
<answer><p><m>8x-12</m>, <m>-2x-10</m>, and <m>-6x+12</m>, respectively.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-monomial-powers">
<statement><p>Expand <m>3x(2x^2-4x+5)</m>. How many products do you need?</p></statement>
<answer><p>Three products: <m>6x^3-12x^2+15x</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-monomial-reverse">
<statement><p>Write <m>6x+18</m> as a product of a monomial and a bracket. What mathematical law justifies your answer?</p></statement>
<answer><p><m>6x+18=6(x+3)</m>, by the distributive law in reverse.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-monomial-reflection">
<title>Check your understanding</title>
<p>What must be multiplied when a bracket contains three terms? Can you explain why <m>2(x+3)</m> is not <m>2x+3</m>? Can you expand without drawing arrows once you understand the law?</p>
</paragraphs>
<p>Practise first with a numerical monomial, then with a variable and signed coefficients. The same law applies in every case.</p>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-two-brackets">
<title>Two brackets: apply the same law twice</title>
<p>There is no new multiplication law. Treat one bracket as a complete factor and distribute the terms of the other bracket across it.</p>
<me>(a+b)(c+d)=a(c+d)+b(c+d)=ac+ad+bc+bd.</me>
<example xml:id="expanding-two-brackets-example">
<title>Four products, one principle</title>
<statement><p>Expand <m>(2x+3)(4x+5)</m>.</p></statement>
<solution>
<me>\begin{aligned}(2x+3)(4x+5)&amp;=8x^2+10x+12x+15\\&amp;=8x^2+22x+15.\end{aligned}</me>
<p>Four partial products give three unlike terms after the like <m>x</m> terms are collected.</p>
</solution>
</example>
<p><url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/maths/algebra/expansions/" visual="Compare the interactive expansion methods"/>. Use them to understand and check calculations, then work towards the quickest reliable method for you.</p>
</section>


<section xml:id="af3-01-positive-factors-unit-factor">
<title>AF3-01: Trinomial — Positive Factors, Unit Factor</title>
<objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol>
<li><p>Identify the unit coefficients in a factor of the form <m>x+1</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>Expand and factorise using the same mathematical structure.</p></li>
<li><p>Explain a shortcut using the multiplicative identity and the distributive law.</p></li>
</ol></objectives>
<paragraphs xml:id="af3-01-why-start-here"><title>Learning to Learn — Why start here?</title>
<p>There is more than one sensible place to begin. We could begin with the simplest quadratic or the unrestricted general case. Both choices have a cost: the simplest case can hide important structure, while the general case can make the reverse calculation demanding before we have learnt what to look for.</p>
<p>Instead, we begin with a carefully chosen special case, <m>(ax+b)(x+1)</m>. It is already a substantial algebraic expression, but its two unit multipliers reveal a useful relationship between the three coefficients. Expansion is usually easier than factorisation because we know all the factors at the outset. By investigating both directions together, we learn to recognise structures instead of guessing blindly.</p>
<p>We will use important special cases as landmarks, moving towards the general case and returning to simpler cases when useful. The distributive law remains our foundation. The choice of entry point is about learning, not a restriction on the mathematics.</p>
</paragraphs>
<p>Begin with a numerical example. What do you notice when one of the factors is <m>x+1</m>?</p>
<example xml:id="af3-01-numerical"><title>Friends of ten</title><statement><p>Expand <m>(7x+3)(x+1)</m>.</p></statement><solution><me>(7x+3)(x+1)=7x^2+7x+3x+3=7x^2+10x+3.</me><p>The two middle contributions are <m>7x</m> and <m>3x</m>. The leading coefficient is <m>7</m>, the constant term is <m>3</m>, and the middle coefficient is their sum.</p></solution></example>
<figure xml:id="af3-01-three-representations"><caption>Choose inline arcs, display (stacked multiplication), or the area model. Predict the next step, then reveal it.</caption><interactive xml:id="af3-01-interactive" iframe="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/algebra/af3-01-unit-factor/index.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1"/></figure>
<p><url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/algebra/af3-01-unit-factor/index.html" visual="Open the investigation full screen"/>. Change the coefficients, swap the factors, switch to letters, or reverse the direction to factorisation.</p>
<definition xml:id="af3-multiplicative-identity"><title>Multiplicative identity</title><statement><p>The number <m>1</m> is the <term>multiplicative identity</term>: multiplying any number by <m>1</m> leaves it unchanged. <m>k\cdot1=k</m>.</p></statement></definition>
<p><idx>unit factor</idx><idx>cross-products</idx>In this investigation, we call <m>x+1</m> the unit factor because both its coefficients are <m>1</m>. This is a descriptive name for this case, not a claim that <m>x+1</m> is a unit in the polynomial ring.</p>
<example xml:id="af3-01-symbolic"><title>From numbers to letters</title><statement><p>Expand <m>(ax+b)(x+1)</m>, where <m>a,b</m> are positive integers.</p></statement><solution><me>\begin{aligned}(ax+b)(x+1)&amp;=ax^2+ax+bx+b\\&amp;=ax^2+(a+b)x+b.\end{aligned}</me><p>The cross-products have unit coefficient multipliers: <m>a\cdot1=a</m> and <m>b\cdot1=b</m>. One middle contribution comes from the leading term, the other from the constant term. Both contribute to the coefficient of <m>x</m>.</p></solution></example>
<paragraphs xml:id="af3-01-shortcut"><title>Insight: the shortcut</title><p>Keep the first coefficient and the constant. Add them to obtain the middle coefficient. For example, <m>(11x+89)(x+1)=11x^2+100x+89</m>. Swapping the factors does not change the product.</p></paragraphs>
<example xml:id="af3-01-yardsticks"><title>Two yardsticks: arranging the same coefficients</title>
<statement><p>Compare <m>(7x+3)(x+1)</m> and <m>(7x+1)(x+3)</m>. What changes? What stays the same?</p></statement>
<solution><me>\begin{aligned}(7x+3)(x+1)&amp;=7x^2+10x+3,\\(7x+1)(x+3)&amp;=7x^2+22x+3.\end{aligned}</me><p>The leading and constant coefficients remain unchanged. The first middle coefficient is a sum, <m>7+3</m>. The second is a product plus one, <m>7\cdot3+1</m>.</p><me>\begin{aligned}(ax+b)(x+1)&amp;=ax^2+(a+b)x+b,\\(ax+1)(x+b)&amp;=ax^2+(ab+1)x+b.\end{aligned}</me><p>For positive integers <m>a,b</m>, <m>ab+1-(a+b)=(a-1)(b-1)\geq0</m>. Thus the sum is the smaller or equal middle coefficient of these two arrangements. The product plus one is the largest possible positive integer middle coefficient when the leading and constant coefficients are fixed at <m>a,b</m>: the two positive integer cross-products multiply to <m>ab</m>, and their sum cannot exceed <m>ab+1</m>.</p><p>The sum <m>a+b</m> need not be the smallest among all possible factorisations with those exterior coefficients. For instance, <m>(12x+3)(x+1)</m> has middle coefficient <m>15</m>, whereas <m>(6x+3)(2x+1)</m> has middle coefficient <m>12</m>. We will investigate these other arrangements later.</p></solution></example>
<paragraphs xml:id="af3-01-geometric"><title>Geometrical interpretation</title><p>For positive <m>x</m>, consider a rectangle with sides <m>ax+b</m> and <m>x+1</m>. Its four component areas are <m>ax^2,ax,bx,b</m>. Combining the two middle areas gives <m>(a+b)x</m>. The area model illustrates the identity for positive lengths; the algebraic identity itself is valid for every real <m>x</m>.</p></paragraphs>
<example xml:id="af3-01-reverse"><title>Reverse the process</title><statement><p>Factorise <m>6x^2+10x+4</m>.</p></statement><solution><p>Since <m>6+4=10</m>, recognise the unit-factor pattern.</p><me>6x^2+10x+4=(6x+4)(x+1).</me><p>Check by expanding. If asked to factorise fully, also extract the common factor: <m>2(3x+2)(x+1)</m>.</p></solution></example>
<exercise xml:id="af3-01-expand"><title>Expand — six variations</title><statement><ol><li><p><m>(7x+3)(x+1)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(ax+b)(x+1)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(11x+89)(x+1)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(x+1)(7x+3)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(x+1)(cx+d)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(x+1)(13x+17)</m></p></li></ol></statement><answer><ol><li><p><m>7x^2+10x+3</m></p></li><li><p><m>ax^2+(a+b)x+b</m></p></li><li><p><m>11x^2+100x+89</m></p></li><li><p><m>7x^2+10x+3</m></p></li><li><p><m>cx^2+(c+d)x+d</m></p></li><li><p><m>13x^2+30x+17</m></p></li></ol></answer></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="af3-01-factor"><title>Factorise — the same six variations in reverse</title><statement><p>Write each expression as a product of two linear factors. Check by expanding.</p><ol><li><p><m>6x^2+10x+4</m></p></li><li><p><m>px^2+(p+q)x+q</m></p></li><li><p><m>23x^2+100x+77</m></p></li><li><p><m>8x^2+20x+12</m></p></li><li><p><m>rx^2+(r+s)x+s</m></p></li><li><p><m>14x^2+30x+16</m></p></li></ol></statement><answer><ol><li><p><m>(6x+4)(x+1)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(px+q)(x+1)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(23x+77)(x+1)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(x+1)(8x+12)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(x+1)(rx+s)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(x+1)(14x+16)</m></p></li></ol></answer></exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="af3-01-learning-to-learn"><title>Learning to Learn — Learning to Teach</title><p>We begin with a numerical example, then use letters to explain why the pattern works. Once we see the structure, we can shorten the calculation. We immediately reverse it through factorisation, rather than waiting until another chapter. Try explaining the shortcut to a classmate, including why it does not apply to every pair of linear factors.</p><p>Use the method that helps you see the mathematics. The aim is to understand the relationship, not to repeat every representation on every question.</p></paragraphs>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-distribution">
<title>Recognising every partial product</title>
<p>The partial-product view below reinforces the distributive law. It is a useful explanation, but it need not become your permanent written procedure.</p>
<example xml:id="expanding-example-distribution">
<title>Multiply every term</title>
<me>(2x+3)(4x+5)=8x^2+10x+12x+15=8x^2+22x+15.</me>
<p>There are four partial products but only three different powers of <m>x</m>, because the middle two are like terms.</p>
</example>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-prerequisites">
<statement><p>Without expanding the brackets, predict the leading coefficient, constant term, and number of partial products in <m>(3x+4)(2x+7)</m>. Explain how you know.</p></statement>
<answer><p>The leading coefficient is <m>3\cdot2=6</m>; the constant term is <m>4\cdot7=28</m>; there are <m>2\cdot2=4</m> partial products.</p></answer>
</exercise>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-foil-lab">
<title>FOIL lab: build understanding</title>
<p>In this lab, <em>predict</em> before calculating. Use a different colour to trace each product if you find that helpful. Record your work clearly; large writing and plenty of space are more useful than squeezing the calculation into a small box.</p>
<p>For the product of two binomials, the mnemonic FOIL stands for <em>First, Outer, Inner, Last</em>. It describes four applications of the distributive law. It is not a separate mathematical law.</p>
<example xml:id="expanding-foil-example">
<title>Trace the four products</title>
<p>Use <m>(2x+3)(4x+5)</m>. Predict each product before revealing or calculating it.</p>
<me>\begin{aligned}
\text{First: }&amp;(2x)(4x)=8x^2\\
\text{Outer: }&amp;(2x)(5)=10x\\
\text{Inner: }&amp;(3)(4x)=12x\\
\text{Last: }&amp;(3)(5)=15
\end{aligned}</me>
<p>Collect the two like terms: <m>10x+12x=22x</m>.</p>
</example>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-foil-predict">
<statement><p>Before expanding <m>(3x+2)(5x+4)</m>, predict the First and Last products mentally. Then find Outer and Inner, and collect like terms.</p></statement>
<answer><p>First <m>15x^2</m>, Outer <m>12x</m>, Inner <m>10x</m>, Last <m>8</m>. The result is <m>15x^2+22x+8</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-foil-pairs">
<statement>
<p>Two brackets have two terms each. Write down all the term pairs before calculating anything. Explain how you can tell that none has been missed or counted twice.</p>
<p>Repeat for <m>(2x+3)(4x^2+5x+6)</m>. What changes?</p>
</statement>
<answer><p>The first pair gives four pairs because <m>2\cdot2=4</m>. In the second expression each of the two linear terms pairs with all three quadratic terms, giving <m>2\cdot3=6</m>. List them systematically, one factor at a time.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-foil-limits">
<statement><p>Try to use the four FOIL labels for <m>(2x+3)(x^2+4x+5)</m>. What difficulty arises? How many partial products are required?</p></statement>
<answer><p>There are six partial products, not four. The four-letter mnemonic no longer covers them. General distribution or place-value organisation still works.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-foil-reflect"><title>Reflect</title>
<p>FOIL can help you <em>see</em> the four products and avoid omissions. Once you understand distribution, naming all four products can become slower than grouping the middle contributions immediately. Moreover, FOIL does not name all six products when a quadratic factor is present. The important idea to keep is <em>every term by every term</em>, not a four-letter mnemonic.</p>
</paragraphs>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-cross-products">
<title>The cross-product method: collect like terms in a margin</title>
<p>For two binomials, a compact alternative is to multiply the leading terms and constant terms directly, then combine the two cross-products for the middle coefficient.</p>
<me>\begin{aligned}
(ax+b)(cx+d)&amp;=acx^2+(ad+bc)x+bd,\\
\text{middle-coefficient margin: }&amp;ad+bc.
\end{aligned}</me>
<example xml:id="expanding-cross-example">
<title>Three coefficients, one margin calculation</title>
<p>Expand <m>(3x+4)(5x+2)</m>. Predict the outside coefficients first.</p>
<me>\begin{aligned}
\text{Leading: }&amp;3\cdot5=15,\\
\text{Margin (cross-products): }&amp;3\cdot2+4\cdot5=6+20=26,\\
\text{Constant: }&amp;4\cdot2=8.
\end{aligned}</me>
<me>(3x+4)(5x+2)=15x^2+26x+8.</me>
<p>The margin collects the contributions to the <m>x</m> column immediately, avoiding an extra line of working. This is still exactly the distributive law; it simply organises the arithmetic more efficiently.</p>
</example>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-cross-predict">
<statement><p>Before writing anything, can you predict the coefficient of <m>x^2</m> and the constant term in <m>(4x+3)(2x+5)</m>? Now put the two cross-products into a small margin and work out the missing coefficient.</p></statement>
<answer><p>Leading coefficient <m>8</m>; constant <m>15</m>; margin <m>4\cdot5+3\cdot2=20+6=26</m>. The result is <m>8x^2+26x+15</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-cross-try">
<statement><p>Use the cross-product margin to expand <m>(2x+5)(3x+4)</m>. Write the leading coefficient, margin calculation and constant term.</p></statement>
<answer><p>Leading <m>6</m>; margin <m>2\cdot4+5\cdot3=8+15=23</m>; constant <m>20</m>. Therefore <m>6x^2+23x+20</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-cross-reflect"><title>Reflect</title>
<p>Can you predict the leading and constant coefficients without pen and paper? Which two cross-products must be combined in the margin? Explain why a single middle-coefficient margin is specific to two linear factors. The distributive law itself remains valid for higher-degree factors.</p>
</paragraphs>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-place-value">
<title>Place-value multiplication: when a quadratic is involved</title>
<p>For a linear factor multiplied by a quadratic, each term of the linear factor must multiply all three terms of the quadratic. Align partial products under their powers of <m>x</m> and then add coefficients in the same column.</p>
<example xml:id="expanding-place-value-example">
<title>Six partial products</title>
<me>\begin{aligned}
(2x+3)(4x^2+5x+6)
&amp;=8x^3+10x^2+12x+12x^2+15x+18\\
&amp;=8x^3+22x^2+27x+18.
\end{aligned}</me>
<p>Try calculating the bottom constant's full row before moving to the bottom <m>x</m> term. The place-value columns organise terms with the same power of <m>x</m>.</p>
</example>
<p><url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/maths/apps/polynomial-place-value/" visual="Expanding Brackets interactive app"/> lets you work through partial products one by one, group like terms, and reflect on your result.</p>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-leading-constant">
<statement><p>For <m>(3x+5)(2x^2+4x+7)</m>, predict the leading coefficient and constant term first. Then calculate the middle coefficients using aligned partial products.</p></statement>
<answer><p>The leading coefficient is <m>6</m> and the constant term is <m>35</m>. The expansion is <m>6x^3+22x^2+41x+35</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-homework">
<title>Homework: consolidate, assess and extend</title>
<p>Complete eight fully worked questions in your Maths copy using the interactive app.</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Select <em>Linear × Linear</em>; complete the four numbered Homework questions.</p></li>
<li><p>Select <em>Linear × Quadratic</em>; complete the four numbered Homework questions.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>You have the whole weekend for these eight questions. Use <em>Step by step</em> to understand and check your working: that support is there to be used. Read the reflection after each question. Then assess your own understanding with quizzes, which give feedback along the way, and tests, which report results at the end after submission.</p>
<p>Practise to develop confidence, enjoyment, accuracy and fluency. A screenshot of a quiz or test result may be shared privately with your teacher if you wish; it is not a substitute for the eight fully worked questions.</p>
<p>You may try extra questions or investigations if you want further challenge. Make a note of any misconception you identify and how you corrected it.</p>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-revisit-plan"><title>Assess yourself and plan to revisit</title>
<p>After an exercise, rate how much help you used from <m>1</m> (very little) to <m>5</m> (a lot). Rate again how much help you expect to need next time. These are reflections, not marks. Aim to reduce the support you need as your confidence grows.</p>
<p>Revisit a similar problem tomorrow, quiz yourself a week later, and take a short test after a month. Attempt each question from memory before checking with the app. If you find a mistake, correct it, identify the cause, and practise that skill again sooner.</p>
</paragraphs>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-investigate-common">
<statement><p>Investigate whether expanding <m>(10x+15)(2x^2+3x+4)</m> becomes easier if you first write <m>10x+15=5(2x+3)</m>. Where must the factor <m>5</m> go at the end?</p></statement>
<answer><p>Expand <m>(2x+3)(2x^2+3x+4)=4x^3+12x^2+17x+12</m>, then multiply every coefficient by <m>5</m>, giving <m>20x^3+60x^2+85x+60</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-investigate-negative">
<statement><p>Show that <m>7-3x=-(3x-7)</m>. Explain how this allows a positive leading coefficient inside the bracket and what you must do to the expanded answer at the end.</p></statement>
<answer><p>Multiplying <m>3x-7</m> by <m>-1</m> gives <m>-3x+7</m>. If this expression occurs as a factor in a product, expand using <m>3x-7</m> and then negate every coefficient of the answer.</p></answer>
</exercise>
</section>
<section xml:id="expanding-scaling">
<title>Investigation lab: scaling a factor scales the product</title>
<p>There is one idea behind several useful tricks of the trade: if one factor is multiplied by a number, then the product is multiplied by that same number.</p>
<me>\bigl(kA(x)\bigr)B(x)=A(x)\bigl(kB(x)\bigr)=k\bigl(A(x)B(x)\bigr).</me>
<p>You can scale either factor. More generally, scaling the two factors by <m>r</m> and <m>s</m> scales the answer by <m>rs</m>.</p>
<me>\bigl(rA(x)\bigr)\bigl(sB(x)\bigr)=rs\bigl(A(x)B(x)\bigr).</me>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-scale-double">
<statement>
<p>Expand <m>(2x+3)(x+4)</m>. Now double <em>both</em> coefficients of the linear factor so that it becomes <m>4x+6</m>. Keep the second bracket unchanged and compare the results.</p>
<p>Could you instead have taken out a common factor <m>2</m>, expanded the original brackets and simply doubled every coefficient in the answer? Predict what would happen with a factor of <m>3</m>, or any number <m>k</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer><p>The first product is <m>2x^2+11x+12</m>. The doubled factor gives <m>(4x+6)(x+4)=4x^2+22x+24</m>, exactly twice the first result. In general <m>(kA)B=k(AB)</m>, because multiplication is associative and commutative.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-scale-either-factor">
<statement>
<p>Does it matter <em>which factor</em> you double? Compare <m>(4x+6)(x+4)</m> with <m>(2x+3)(2x+8)</m>. Predict their expansions from <m>(2x+3)(x+4)</m> before multiplying.</p>
<p>Try tripling either factor. Then double the first factor and triple the second. By what number is the original answer multiplied?</p>
</statement>
<answer><p>Both doubled products are <m>4x^2+22x+24</m>. Tripling either factor gives <m>6x^2+33x+36</m>. Doubling one and tripling the other produces six times the original product: <m>12x^2+66x+72</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-scale-hcf">
<statement><p>Before expanding <m>(6x+9)(4x+8)</m>, take out the highest common factor from <em>each</em> bracket. What numerical factor can you place in front of the simpler product? Why might this be efficient?</p></statement>
<answer><p><m>(6x+9)(4x+8)=3(2x+3)\cdot4(x+2)=12(2x+3)(x+2)=24x^2+84x+72</m>. Taking out the numerical factors first reveals the structure and reduces the sizes of the numbers being multiplied.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-scale-negative">
<statement>
<p>Now change the signs of <em>both</em> terms in the factor <m>7x-1</m>, giving <m>-7x+1</m>. Multiply each factor by <m>x+2</m>. Before calculating the second product, predict every coefficient of its answer.</p>
<p>Explain why changing all the signs is the special case <m>k=-1</m> of scaling a factor.</p>
</statement>
<answer><p><m>(7x-1)(x+2)=7x^2+13x-2</m>. Since <m>-7x+1=-(7x-1)</m>, the new product is <m>-7x^2-13x+2</m>, the negative of the original answer.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-scale-common">
<statement><p>Investigate <m>(10x+15)(2x^2+3x+4)</m>. First write <m>10x+15=5(2x+3)</m>. Expand the smaller factors, then scale the answer by <m>5</m>. Is it less work?</p></statement>
<answer><p><m>(2x+3)(2x^2+3x+4)=4x^3+12x^2+17x+12</m>. Therefore the required product is <m>20x^3+60x^2+85x+60</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-scaling-reflect"><title>Reflect and connect to factorising</title>
<p>Why does it not matter which factor is doubled? Why must <em>every</em> coefficient of a scaled factor change, not just one? Why does scaling both factors by <m>2</m> multiply the answer by <m>4</m>, not <m>2</m>? Taking out the highest common factor first often simplifies expansion and makes numerical factors explicit, which is especially useful when we begin factorising.</p>
</paragraphs>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-zero-placeholders">
<title>Hidden Maths: do we need the zeros?</title>
<p>In place-value multiplication, a zero can hold an otherwise empty position. We sometimes omit zero terms when writing a simplified polynomial, just as we usually omit the positive sign at the beginning of a positive expression. But the risks are different when arranging a multiplication.</p>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-zero-investigation">
<statement>
<p>Multiply <m>(2x+3)(4x^2+5x+6)</m> using partial-product rows. Write the shifted rows with zero placeholders. Then try arranging the same rows without writing the zeros.</p>
<p>Which presentation makes the powers of <m>x</m> easier to align? What mistake could you make if you simply move a partial-product row to the left without keeping track of its place value?</p>
<p>When you are very confident, can you leave the zero symbols out <em>without</em> losing the correct columns? Explain how you will check your work.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>One arrangement uses the coefficient rows <m>(12,15,18)</m> under the <m>x^2</m>, <m>x</m> and constant columns, and <m>(8,10,12,0)</m> under <m>x^3</m>, <m>x^2</m>, <m>x</m> and constant. Column addition gives <m>8x^3+22x^2+27x+18</m>. The zero is a placeholder, not an additional non-zero product. You can omit its glyph only if the blank position and column alignment are unambiguous.</p>
</answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-zero-reflection"><title>Reflect: do so at your own risk</title>
<p>In a final simplified expression, we normally omit zero terms, but while learning to multiply, write zero placeholders in shifted rows. Do not confuse a missing coefficient with a shift in place value. Only consider leaving the zeros unwritten once you can reliably maintain alignment and check your result.</p>
</paragraphs>
<p>Historical connection: zero as a placeholder is ancient, but its use as a number developed over time. Different civilisations devised placeholder symbols independently. Indian mathematician Brahmagupta described arithmetic involving zero in <m>628</m> CE; the Hindu-Arabic place-value system subsequently spread through the Islamic world into Europe. Zero was <em>not</em> invented just two or three hundred years ago.</p>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-language">
<title>Mathematical language: say it, use it, explain it</title>
<p>Use these words when explaining your work. For the wider vocabulary of algebra, see <xref ref="algebra-vocabulary"/>.</p>
<dl>
<li><title>Binomial</title><p>A polynomial with exactly two non-zero terms, such as <m>3x+2</m>.</p></li>
<li><title>Quadratic</title><p>A polynomial of degree <m>2</m>, such as <m>x^2+5</m>. A quadratic does not have to contain an <m>x</m> term.</p></li>
<li><title>Monic</title><p>A polynomial whose leading coefficient is exactly <m>1</m>, such as <m>x^2+7x+12</m>. Monic does not mean that all the other coefficients are <m>1</m>, or that a question is necessarily easy.</p></li>
<li><title>Leading coefficient</title><p>The coefficient of the highest power, such as <m>4</m> in <m>4x^2-3x+1</m>.</p></li>
<li><title>Distributive law</title><p>The property <m>a(b+c)=ab+ac</m>, used repeatedly in expansion.</p></li>
<li><title>Partial product</title><p>One contribution obtained by multiplying a term from each factor.</p></li>
<li><title>Like terms</title><p>Terms with matching variable parts and powers, such as <m>3x^2</m> and <m>-5x^2</m>.</p></li>
<li><title>Sparse polynomial</title><p>A polynomial with one or more zero coefficients between non-zero terms, such as <m>x^2+9</m>, whose <m>x</m> coefficient is <m>0</m>.</p></li>
<li><title>Quintic</title><p>A polynomial of degree <m>5</m>. This is a useful name to recognise, but fifth-degree expansion is not required for the current homework.</p></li>
</dl>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-language-sort">
<statement><p>Decide which expressions are monic and which are quadratics: <m>x^2+4</m>, <m>2x^2+1</m>, <m>x^3-x</m>, <m>3x+7</m>. Can one expression be both monic and quadratic? Explain.</p></statement>
<answer><p><m>x^2+4</m> is both monic and quadratic; <m>2x^2+1</m> is quadratic but not monic; <m>x^3-x</m> is monic and cubic; <m>3x+7</m> is linear and not monic.</p></answer>
</exercise>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-hidden-maths">
<title>Hidden Maths: patterns worth discovering</title>
<p>A good investigation begins with a prediction. Look for a pattern in the structure before carrying out all the arithmetic.</p>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-hidden-x-plus-one">
<statement>
<p>Multiply each expression by <m>x+1</m>, but first predict what the coefficients of the answer might be:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><m>(2x+3)(x+1)</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(2x^2+3x+4)(x+1)</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(3x^2+5x+2)(x+1)</m></p></li>
</ol>
<p>Investigate: Which coefficients are unchanged? Which come from adding two neighbouring coefficients? How would the pattern change for <m>x-1</m>? Can you describe a rule that works for any polynomial?</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The answers are <m>2x^2+5x+3</m>, <m>2x^3+5x^2+7x+4</m>, and <m>3x^3+8x^2+7x+2</m>.</p>
<p>Write the coefficients of <m>P(x)</m> in descending powers and imagine a zero at either end. Multiplication by <m>x+1</m> adds each coefficient to the neighbouring coefficient one place to its right. The leading and constant coefficients remain unchanged. Multiplication by <m>x-1</m> instead gives adjacent differences, with the constant term changing sign.</p>
</answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-hidden-reflect">
<title>Reflect</title>
<p>Could you multiply by <m>x+1</m> by working with the coefficients alone, without repeatedly writing powers of <m>x</m>? Why does the place-value method make this pattern so visible?</p>
</paragraphs>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-beyond-school">
<title>Beyond school maths: an optional quintic challenge</title>
<p>A quartic polynomial has degree <m>4</m>, and a <term>quintic</term> has degree <m>5</m>. These names describe degrees, not new multiplication rules. The same distributive law still works.</p>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-quintic-exploration">
<statement>
<p>Optional challenge: Without multiplying each term in a long, separate calculation, predict the degree and then expand <m>(x+1)(x^5+2x^3+3)</m>. Can the place-value method help you keep track of the missing powers?</p>
</statement>
<answer><p>The answer has degree <m>6</m> and equals <m>x^6+x^5+2x^4+2x^3+3x+3</m>. Treat the missing powers in the quintic factor as having zero coefficients when aligning the partial products.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<p>This is an extension, not part of the eight compulsory homework questions. Higher-degree multiplication can be explored later without adding more controls to the main classroom app.</p>
</section>

